---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro"

const docStructure = `system "AUTHORITY:lang-SrcScript:DstScript:year" {
  metadata {
    authority_id odni
    id 2004
    language iso-639-3:prs
    source_script Arab
    destination_script Latn
  }
  stage main {
    parallel {
      sub "ئ" ""
    }
  }
  tests { "input" -> "expected" }
}`

const rules = `sub { from "a" + any("bc") to "X" before "d" after boundary }
maybe("h")            // zero-or-one, participates in longest-match
capture("a") + "b"    // captured; ref(1) substitutes it in the target
any("0" .. "9")       // inclusive codepoint range
any_character         // any single character`

const aliases = `aliases { vowel = any("aeiou") }
stage main { parallel { sub { from "b" + vowel + "t" to "X" } } }`

const run = `run stage.main              // this document
run map.dep.stage.main      // a declared dependency

downcase                    // stage-level stdlib directives run at their
upcase                      // rule position on the whole string
title_case
compose / decompose
separate / unseparate`

const casing = `sub { from "ة" to upcase }   // uppercases the matched text only`

const tests = `tests { "input" -> "expected" }`
---

<Base
  title="The ISC Language — Interscript"
  description="The normative reference for ISC, the declarative specification language for script conversion (IS-1 v2)."
>
  <h1 class="mb-6 text-3xl font-bold">The ISC Language</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      ISC (Interscript Specification Code) is the declarative language for
      script conversion. A <code>.isc</code> file denotes exactly one
      conversion function; every runtime computes the same object. This is
      the normative language reference (IS-1 v2 — the executable form is
      the 22-vector suite in interscript-ruby).
    </p>

    <h2>Document structure</h2>
    <pre><code>{docStructure}</code></pre>

    <h2>Stages</h2>
    <p><code>sequence</code> executes rules in order. <code>parallel</code> is
    single-pass longest-match — the longest applicable rule wins at each
    position; duplicate rules resolve last-declared-wins.</p>

    <h2>Rules and contexts</h2>
    <pre><code>{rules}</code></pre>
    <p><code>before</code>/<code>after</code> are zero-width
    lookbehind/lookahead. <code>boundary</code> is a zero-width
    word edge. <code>not_before</code>/<code>not_after</code> negate. <strong>There is no alternation operator</strong> — alternatives are separate rules.</p>

    <h2>Aliases</h2>
    <pre><code>{aliases}</code></pre>
    <p>Aliases bind named item expressions at parse time and are usable in
    both from and to positions.</p>

    <h2>Stages calling stages; stdlib directives</h2>
    <pre><code>{run}</code></pre>
    <p><code>run</code> takes no other form — stray identifiers
    are a parse error (a typo'd map fails loudly).
    The <code>rababa</code> directive invokes the ML adapter with
    mandatory keyword arguments; an unprovisioned model raises a named
    error enumerating known ids.</p>

    <h2>Casing targets</h2>
    <pre><code>{casing}</code></pre>

    <h2>Embedded tests</h2>
    <pre><code>{tests}</code></pre>
    <p>Normative data on the document — executed by the verification layer
    (the conformance runner), never by <code>transliterate</code>.</p>

    <h2>The strict parse</h2>
    <p>The corpus is strict-parse-clean: stray identifiers and undeclared
    constructs are parse errors. A typo'd map fails loudly.</p>

    <h2>Normative reference</h2>
    <p>The full specification (IS-1 v2) with the 22 frozen rulings and the
    cross-runtime guarantee lives in <a
    href="https://github.com/interscript/interscript-models/blob/main/docs/IS-1-v2.md">interscript-models/docs/IS-1-v2.md</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
